  "answer": "I'll start by inspecting the workspace.\n**Fixed `src/calc.ts`.**\n\nThe bug was a type inconsistency: the running total was annotated as `string` even though `reduce(..., 0)` on `number[]` produces a `number`. The code then papered over the mismatch by wrapping the result in `Number(...)` at return time.\n\nChange (only two lines touched):\n```ts\nconst total: number = values.reduce((sum, value) => sum + value, 0)\nif (values.length === 0) return 0\nreturn total / values.length\n```\n- `total` is now a `number`, matching what `reduce` actually returns.\n- The `Number(total)` coercion is gone, since `total` is already numeric.\n- Exported signature `average(values: number[]): number` unchanged; empty-input guard and behavior preserved.\n\nVerification:\n- `bun test` → 1 pass, 0 fail (2 expect calls) — `average([2,4]) === 3`, `average([]) === 0`.\n- `bunx tsc --noEmit --strict` on `src/calc.ts` → exit 0, no diagnostics (tsc 5.8.2).\n\nUntouched as required: `AGENTS.md`, `package.json`, `test/oracle.test.ts`; no config added and the Bun test runner was kept."
